abstract: >-
  Tests whether species-richness estimators from ecology (Chao1, iChao1, ACE, jackknife, sample coverage) can tell
  how many failure types of a classical machine-learning model remain undiscovered after testing. Planted failures
  (flipped labels in rare subgroups) and natural failures (errors of a clean model grouped into types fixed in
  advance) give the truth. In a pre-registered main study (5 tabular datasets, 3 model families, 450 jobs), the share
  of errors from not-yet-seen failure types was estimated without bias in 95.6% of planted and 100% of natural cells,
  with an honest 95% interval for natural failures (95.6% of cells) but not planted ones (71.1%). The number of
  failure types could not be estimated reliably and is best reported as a lower bound.
keywords:
  - machine learning testing
  - failure modes
  - species richness
  - Chao1
  - tabular data
  - sample coverage
  - pre-registration
